{
  "requirement": "When given `--path <crate-dir>` and no existing Habeas dev dependency, `habeas setup` MUST add that local crate as the dev dependency. Repeating setup MUST NOT add a duplicate Habeas dependency.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The tests have some weak spots, listed at the end.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/setup.rs`, `has_habeas_dev_dependency`, returns true if `[dev-dependencies]` contains a `habeas` key. It uses `as_table_like`, so it covers both the inline form and the `[dev-dependencies.habeas]` form.\n- In `run` (about lines 143–160), `cargo add --dev habeas --manifest-path …` runs only when no such dependency exists.\n- When `--path` is given, `run` appends `--path <canonicalized dir>`. So an absent dependency is added as the local crate, and an existing one is left alone on repeat runs.\n- `cargo add` failures are reported as errors.\n- Both pieces carry `{impl}` annotations for this requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/setup.rs::setup_is_repeatable` is annotated `{test}` for this requirement.\n  - It runs `habeas setup --path $CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` twice on a fresh fixture project that has no Habeas dependency.\n  - It then asserts that `Cargo.toml` has `[dev-dependencies]`, mentions `habeas`, and contains `path = `.\n- Several other tests in `tests/setup.rs` also run `setup --path` against fixtures.\n- `tests/cli.rs` checks that invalid `--path` argument combinations are rejected.\n- `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py` runs `setup --path` on a project that already has a path dev-dependency, then confirms the setup advisory stops.\n\n**Gaps (minor; I did not treat them as failures)**\n- The test does not count `habeas` entries or compare `Cargo.toml` between the first and second runs. It also does not check that the `path` value points at the supplied crate.\n- No test starts with a pre-existing, different Habeas dependency and asserts it is untouched. A duplicate key cannot exist in a TOML table, and the guard is a short, obviously correct check, so these gaps are low risk.\n- No source comment acknowledges these gaps, so there is no FIXME to cite.",
